(11. SigV4 authentication is surprisingly complicated) Tj
T*
(Source: Xe Iaso's blog | Published: Thu, 06 Aug 2026 00:00:00 GMT) Tj
T*
(Link: https://www.tigrisdata.com/blog/sigv4/) Tj
T*
(SigV4 looks simple: sign a request, check the signature. Then you implement canonicalization,) Tj
T*
(clock skew, and a cache that isn't allowed to hold your key. Tigris is a drop-in replacement) Tj
T*
(for AWS S3 \(or GCS, anything S3API compatible\). As such, we need to be fully compatible with) Tj
T*
(both the mechanisms and semantics of S3 including the SigV4 authentication protocol . This is) Tj
T*
(the lingua franca of authentication in the object storage landscape; even Google Cloud Storage) Tj
T*
(has a way to enable SigV4 support so you can use existing applications against its object) Tj
T*
(storage service. At first I thought that SigV4 was fairly simple. Clients sign requests,) Tj
T*
(servers do the same work and make sure the result matches. The main sticking point is that the) Tj
T*
(cryptography involved is symmetric cryptography, the kind where both parties need to have the) Tj
T*
(same secrets. This makes some scaling issues weird, but we'll get into that in the future. Note) Tj
T*
(This is only going to be talking about authentication \(ensuring the identity of a remote) Tj
T*
(client\), not authorization \(ensuring the client has the permission to do something\).) Tj
T*
(Authorization will come in the future for reasons that will become obvious when you see that...) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
